My first attempt at this commit also collapsed the body and notes
limits from soft-72/hard-120 down to a hard 72, on the theory that the
skill and the hook were two sources of truth and the looser one was
winning. That was wrong twice over. 35d32b9 introduced 120
deliberately, as room for code snippets, benchmark output, and long
identifiers that do not wrap, and the hook is a trigger rather than a
ban — it stops you and makes you decide, and --no-verify is the
intended way past it for a line that is legitimately long. The two
were never in conflict. I am recording it here because the wrong story
was more persuasive than the right one and someone may reach for it
again.

Whether the hook itself should distinguish the soft limit from the hard
one is a separate question I have not touched. As it stands it fires at
72, so any commit using the 72-to-120 range needs --no-verify.

The skill now depends on infrastructure that is not in the repo: the
hook is untracked and lives in the shared git dir, so it covers this
worktree but nothing outside it. Run peff-commit elsewhere and the
message width has no trigger at all, where before there was at least a
post-hoc grep. A follow-up worth considering is having the skill notice
a missing commit-msg hook and offer to install one; deliberately not
done here.

I considered piping the notes through `fmt -w 72` instead of grepping
them, and rejected it. fmt would enforce the soft limit as if it were
hard, and on this host it reflows rather than wraps — it joins short
lines within a paragraph, even under -s, and double-spaces after
sentence ends — so a pasted table or a deliberate line break in a note
would not survive. Reporting a violation leaves the judgement where it
belongs.

The hook behaviour in the commit message is tested, not assumed: a
throwaway repo with all five commit hooks logging showed every one of
them fire on `git commit` and none on `git notes add`, with
reference-transaction firing on the refs/notes/commits update and
receiving only the old sha, new sha, and refname.
